// Synthetic sample analysis file for testing the arsgrammatica.js and
// cts-urn.js libraries. Two short sentences from the opening of the
// Aeneid, tokenized and (loosely, for test purposes) analyzed in the
// format documented at
// https://neelsmith.github.io/arsgrammatica/reference/analysisformat.html
//
// Sentence 1: "Arma virumque cano." (5 tokens: arma / virum / que / cano / .)
//   - single context; also exercises the "root" sentinel (cano's own
//     related1/relationship1 mark it as the sentence's root verb, not
//     an edge to another token) and an enclitic ("que") with two edges.
//
// Sentence 2: "Troiae qui primus ab oris Italiam fato profugus Laviniaque
//   venit litora." (trimmed here to 6 tokens, to test menu truncation),
//   spanning two contexts (1.2, 1.3). Because a token's `related1`/
//   `related2` is only a bare id (unique only *within* its own context),
//   a token can only name a relation to another token in its *own*
//   context; ab->oris (both in 1.3) is representable this way, but
//   nothing in 1.3 can point back to the sentence's root ("primus", in
//   1.2) — that's a real limitation of the format for sentences that
//   cross a context boundary, not a bug in the library. See
//   notes/library-api.md.

#!sentences
context_begin|first_token|context_end|last_token
urn:cts:latinLit:phi0690.phi003.omar:1.1|t0|urn:cts:latinLit:phi0690.phi003.omar:1.1|t4
urn:cts:latinLit:phi0690.phi003.omar:1.2|t0|urn:cts:latinLit:phi0690.phi003.omar:1.3|t3

#!tokens
context|id|tokentype|text|lemma|verbalunit|related1|relationship1|related2|relationship2
